WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
A "Big Bang Theory" star is stepping into&nbsp;
an iconic sitcom role, and she has a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.400
robe to fill. But what happened to&nbsp;
the original "Night Court" judge?

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.960
The brand new "Night Court" is a sequel series to&nbsp;
the classic sitcom that premiered back in 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.880
and ran until 1992, with 193 episodes.&nbsp;
Both "Night Court" series take place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.240
in a courtroom setting in New York City,&nbsp;
though what makes this premise different is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.960
that "Night Court," as the name implies,&nbsp;
covers the graveyard shift. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.920
that the stories and cases are usually zany&nbsp;
misadventures featuring colorful characters.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.280
Of course, it takes somebody special&nbsp;
to preside over such a courtroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.640
and the new "Night Court" lets Judge&nbsp;
Abby Stone, played by Melissa Rauch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.300
have a tremendous amount of fun, despite&nbsp;
the lofty and often humorless role.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.080
Fans of the original "Night Court" will remember&nbsp;
the judge who held sway in that courtroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.220
Harry Anderson's Judge Harry Stone. Harry&nbsp;
wound up presiding over the original "Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.180
Court" because the Mayor was simply too busy&nbsp;
and overwhelmed doling out positions within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.540
the government, and Harry happened to be&nbsp;
one of the few people to answer his phone.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.380
Harry soon found himself working the night&nbsp;
shift courtroom in Manhattan — though on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.160
account of his young age and unique outlook&nbsp;
on life, his rulings were usually much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.480
personable and understanding than what many may&nbsp;
experience when coming face-to-face with a judge.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.500
The new "Night Court," with Judge Abby Stone&nbsp;
presiding, has already established that Abby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.460
is Harry's daughter, and in one of her first&nbsp;
interactions with series veteran Dan Fielding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.620
played by John Larroquette, the two discuss Harry.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.760
Dan was the prosecutor in the original "Night&nbsp;
Court," meaning that he's exceptionally familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.180
with Harry, as the two had something&nbsp;
of a friendly antagonism. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.840
Dan mentions that although Harry and himself&nbsp;
hadn't been close in quite some time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.140
he offers condolences on Harry's&nbsp;
passing, which is kept vague.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.560
"I was sorry to hear about your father.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.244 --> 00:01:37.560
We had lost touch over the&nbsp;
years, but he was a great guy."

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.520
Actor Harry Anderson was beloved on the original&nbsp;
"Night Court" and other famous shows like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.160
"Cheers," and his death in 2018 at the age of 65&nbsp;
due to a stroke was a real loss for the television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.180
landscape. The new "Night Court" revival&nbsp;
decided that instead of recasting the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.380
they instead would reflect real life with an&nbsp;
off-screen death. This has not been lost on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.640
those who remember the original show, with quite&nbsp;
a few expressing their thoughts on social media.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.580
On Reddit, fans tried to place&nbsp;
some of the seemingly random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.940
items delivered to Judge Abby Stone's&nbsp;
office, with one viewer pointing out,

00:02:21.103 --> 00:02:25.123
Another fan on Reddit brought up the rapid fire&nbsp;
nature of the jokes on this new "Night Court,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.123 --> 00:02:28.783
and felt like if the show slowed down,&nbsp;
the laughs would be a bit more robust.

00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:32.154
"Your past, your feelings, you've put your&nbsp;
whole life in a box and won't let anyone in."

00:02:32.154 --> 00:02:33.900
"Wait, hold on a second. Am I in the box or am I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.880
putting things in the box?"&nbsp;
"I kind of lost the thread."

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.780
The fan then mentioned that the late Harry&nbsp;
Anderson had great comedic timing, and added,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.960
Still, it seems as if many still&nbsp;
fondly remember Judge Harry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.480
and are happy to see him&nbsp;
referenced in the new show.

